Hutchinson  et al . [ 10 ]  indicated that solute Nb is more powerful in retarding ferrite recrystallization than NbC in precipitate form if the mean particle diameter is larger than 3 nm. Maruyama  et al . [ 23 ]  investigated the interaction of solute niobium and molybdenum with the ferrite grain boundary. Based on three-dimensional atom probe measurements, they concluded that this interaction is larger for solute Nb than for solute Mo, and hence, a larger inhibition effect on grain-boundary migration is expected for solute Nb. This was explained by the elastic interaction between the solute atom and the grain boundary as the difference in atomic size between solute and Fe being 15 pct for Nb and only 9 pct for Mo. In addition, the effective free energy of segregation at the boundary is larger for Nb (− 38 ± 2 kJ/mol) than for Mo (− 28 ± 2 kJ/mol), both values referring to a temperature of 800 °C. [ 23 ]  The attractive interaction between niobium atoms and dislocations in ferrite leading to the formation of a niobium–Cottrell atmosphere was directly observed by atom probe tomography. [ 24 ]  The segregation energy was estimated to be about 42.4 ± 0.7 kJ/mol for a {112}  < 111 > \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
